## The assignment, in plain language

We’re building a small assistant that answers engineers’ questions using **only three research papers**. It should find relevant passages, write an answer with citations, and refuse to guess when the papers don’t contain the answer. The hard part is keeping it useful when requests arrive quickly, the model service slows down or hits limits, and people stop waiting. The assignment expects us to measure those trade-offs and explain the choices we made. :codex-file-citation{path="/Users/yashpratapsingh/Downloads/The_Relay_Assignment_Brief.pdf" purpose="source"}

Think of it as a busy help desk: each question needs research, several model or tool steps, and a checked reply. When many people ask at once, we have to decide who waits, who gets a simpler answer, and when to stop rather than risk sending something wrong.

## The plan, step by step

1. **Set up the project.** Create the folders, starter files, results tables, Python environment, and Git repository. Put the API key in a local `.env` file that Git ignores. The setup script describes this work. :codex-file-citation{path="/Users/yashpratapsingh/Downloads/relay-agent-pack/bootstrap.sh" purpose="source"}

2. **Measure the real model service carefully.** Use a Groq free-tier account to learn its response times and limits. Save the responses and headers so the conclusions come from evidence. Real calls use the account’s daily allowance, so the project instructions require estimating their cost and getting your “go” before each experiment. That’s a workflow rule in the project plan, not a separate requirement stated in the brief. :codex-file-citation{path="/Users/yashpratapsingh/Downloads/relay-agent-pack/CLAUDE.md" purpose="source"}

3. **Build the paper library and search.** Fetch the specified versions of the three papers, keep page numbers, split them into searchable pieces, and try at least three retrieval settings. Measure how often each setting finds useful evidence, how much text it sends to the model, and how long answers take.

4. **Build the answer pipeline.** In everyday terms, it should understand the question, look up evidence, decide whether it can answer, draft a cited reply, and check that reply before showing it. The plan adds tracing and a stand-in service so we can inspect individual steps and simulate slowdowns or failures. The brief requires at least four steps, one tool call, and a branch or loop. :codex-file-citation{path="/Users/yashpratapsingh/Downloads/relay-agent-pack/CLAUDE.md" purpose="source"}

5. **Test what happens under load.** Run scenarios such as steady traffic, a sudden burst, and overload. Use the real service for modest measurements, then use our stand-in for heavier loads. The stand-in imitates behaviors we measured, such as slow replies and rate-limit errors; it lets us study stress without sending every test to the real service.

6. **Collect results and explain the trade-offs.** Produce the required tables and raw results, plus an example showing where one slow request spent its time. The report has a strict two-page limit, so the results need to be concise. It must cover performance, failures, answer quality, retrieval choices, retries, daily-limit behavior, and what we learned. :codex-file-citation{path="/Users/yashpratapsingh/Downloads/The_Relay_Assignment_Brief.pdf" purpose="source"}

The decision template is there to record options, evidence, the recommendation, and what that choice costs. The overrules log is for your own notes about times you disagreed with an assistant or caught an error. :codex-file-citation{path="/Users/yashpratapsingh/Downloads/relay-agent-pack/docs/decisions/0000-template.md" purpose="source"} :codex-file-citation{path="/Users/yashpratapsingh/Downloads/relay-agent-pack/docs/OVERRULES.md" purpose="source"}

## The key idea behind the architecture

We can’t maximize speed, answer quality, low cost, and serving everyone at the same time. For example, searching more passages may improve the evidence but send more tokens and take longer. Retrying a failed call may recover an answer but add delay and use more of the daily allowance. The project is about measuring those effects, choosing what matters most, and being able to defend the choice.

## A few gaps to fix before following the plan

- **The setup script appears unable to finish with the pack as it currently exists.** It tries to copy `.claude/agents/cto.md`, but that file is missing from the project folder. Because the script exits on errors, it will stop at that copy step.
- **The plan is more ambitious than the brief requires.** For example, it asks for 40 candidate questions and lays out several advanced scheduling and retry components, while the brief says to focus on a few techniques and study them deeply. We should protect time for the required measurements and two-page write-up.
- **The two planning documents don’t line up perfectly on review order.** One says to get CTO review before code governed by a decision; the kickoff prompt places some reviews later. We should resolve that before implementation.
- **The assignment PDF is outside the project folder.** The setup script can copy it into the project if given its path as the second argument. :codex-file-citation{path="/Users/yashpratapsingh/Downloads/relay-agent-pack/KICKOFF.md" purpose="source"} :codex-file-citation{path="/Users/yashpratapsingh/Downloads/relay-agent-pack/bootstrap.sh" purpose="source"}

So the project folder is currently an **instruction pack and starter setup**, not the finished assistant. The next sensible step is to resolve the missing CTO file and trim the plan to the brief’s must-haves before building.

Done: [reflection_draft.md](/Users/yashpratapsingh/Downloads/relay-agent-pack/the-relay/docs/reflection_draft.md).

It has sample wording for all three sections, including the discovery story, feedback grounded in [Vaani’s homepage](https://www.vaani.dev/), prompts that mattered, the assistant’s cost-accounting mistake, and your real R2/R3 decision. I kept it separate from `WRITEUP.md`; personalize any lines that don’t sound like your experience.

I used the later 35-question-bank direction and described your role as directing and reviewing the work, rather than claiming you wrote every answer. :codex-annotation{index="1"}
